.TH PRINTF 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
printf \- write formatted output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B printf
.I format
.RI [ argument .\|.\|.]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B printf
writes
.I format
to standard output, substituting each of its
.B %
conversion directives with the corresponding
.I argument
operand in turn. This is the
.B printf
utility, not the C language's
.B printf()
function: every
.I argument
is plain text, and a numeric conversion parses its own argument's text
itself (see
.B "NUMERIC ARGUMENTS"
below).
.PP
If
.I argument
operands remain after
.I format
has been used once,
.I format
is reused as many additional times as needed to consume them all. If a
whole pass over
.I format
consumes no arguments at all (because it contains no conversion
directive, or only
.BR %% ),
.I format
is still processed exactly once even if
.I argument
operands remain, but is not reused a second time.
.PP
Once every
.I argument
operand has been consumed, an extra
.B %c
or
.B %s
conversion is evaluated as if given an empty string, and every other
extra conversion is evaluated as if given zero; both are ordinary,
non-error outcomes.
.SH FORMAT ESCAPES
Within
.I format
itself (not inside a
.B %b
argument \(em see below), a backslash introduces one of the escape
sequences
.BR \e\e ,
.BR \ea ,
.BR \eb ,
.BR \ef ,
.BR \en ,
.BR \er ,
.BR \et ,
.B \ev
or
.RI \e ddd
(one to three octal digits), each producing a single output byte. Any
other backslash sequence is left in the output exactly as written,
including the backslash itself, rather than being silently dropped.
.SH CONVERSIONS
The recognized conversions are
.BR d ,
.BR i ,
.BR o ,
.BR u ,
.BR x ,
.BR X ,
.BR c ,
.BR s ,
.BR b ,
.BR f ,
.BR e ,
.BR g ,
and
.BR % .
Each (other than
.BR %% )
may be preceded by any combination of the flags
.BR \- ,
.BR 0 ,
.BR + ,
space, and
.BR # ,
a decimal field width, and a
.B .
precision, with the same meaning as in the C language's own
.BR printf (3)
conversions. There is no support for an argument-supplied
.RI ( * )
width or precision, and no length modifiers, since every
.I argument
is already plain text rather than a typed value.
.PP
.B %b
is specific to this utility (distinct from
.BR %s ):
it expands backslash escapes within its own
.I argument
using the table described under
.B "%b ESCAPES"
below, rather than writing the argument out literally.
.SH NUMERIC ARGUMENTS
An
.I argument
given to a numeric conversion
.RB ( d , " i" , " o" , " u" , " x" , " X" , " f" , " e" ,
.BR g )
is parsed the same way a C integer or floating-point constant would
be, except that an
.I argument
beginning with a single or double quote is instead taken as the
numeric value of the single character immediately following that
quote. So, for the integer conversions, a leading
.B 0
means octal and a leading
.B 0x
means hexadecimal, exactly as in C source.
.PP
Unlike passing a negative value to an unsigned conversion function, a
leading
.B \-
given to
.BR %o ,
.BR %u ,
.BR %x ,
or
.B %X
is a diagnosed error, not silently reinterpreted as a very large
unsigned value.
.PP
An
.I argument
that fails to parse for the numeric conversion it was given to (and is
not simply an exhausted argument, see above) is a diagnosed error: a
message is written to standard error, the value zero is used in its
place so the rest of the output still prints something coherent, and
the exit status is forced nonzero \(em but the rest of
.I format
(and any further passes over it) still runs. An unrecognized
conversion letter after
.B %
is a stronger error: it stops the entire invocation immediately,
including any remaining passes over
.IR format ,
and also forces a nonzero exit status.
.SH "%b ESCAPES"
Within a
.B %b
argument, the recognized escapes are
.BR \e\e ,
.BR \ea ,
.BR \eb ,
.BR \ef ,
.BR \en ,
.BR \er ,
.BR \et ,
.BR \ev ,
.B \ec
and
.RI \e0 ddd
(zero to three octal digits after the
.BR 0 ).
This table is distinct from the format-string escapes above in two
ways: plain
.RI \e ddd
(without a leading
.BR 0 )
is not recognized inside a
.B %b
argument, and
.B \ec
is: it stops the entire invocation immediately, discarding the rest of
.I format
and any remaining
.I argument
operands, exactly as an unrecognized conversion letter does.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I format
A string containing ordinary text, escape sequences, and conversion
directives, as described above.
.TP
.I argument
A string consumed, in order, by each conversion directive in
.I format
as it is reused (see above).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every conversion in every pass over
.I format
succeeded, and every
.I argument
that was consumed by a numeric conversion parsed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
.I format
was not given at all, an
.I argument
failed to parse for the numeric conversion it was given to, an
unrecognized conversion letter was used, or a write to standard output
failed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR echo (1)
